# Logic: Arguments, Validity, and the Art of Reasoning

> Logic studies the skeleton of reasoning: how premises support
> conclusions, which forms of inference preserve truth, and the
> recurring patterns by which arguments fail.

## 1. What Is an Argument?

In philosophy, an **argument** is not a quarrel. It is a set of claims —
the **premises** — offered as reasons for accepting one further claim,
the **conclusion**.

All mammals are warm-blooded (premise); all whales are mammals
(premise); therefore, all whales are warm-blooded (conclusion).

Three kinds of inference dominate Western and Indian logic alike:

| Kind | Movement | Standard of success | Example |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Deductive | Rule to case | Necessity — conclusion must follow | All humans are mortal; Socrates is human |
| Inductive | Cases to rule | Probability — strong or weak | Every swan observed so far is white |
| Abductive | Effect to cause | Plausibility — best explanation | The lawn is wet; probably it rained |

[definition]
**Deduction** applies rules with necessity. **Induction** generalises
from observed cases, yielding only probability. **Abduction** infers
the best explanation of an effect.
[/definition]

@diagram tree id="philosophy-logic-inference" layout="tree" aria="Three kinds of inference with their branches"
  "Inference" -> Deductive
  "Inference" -> Inductive
  "Inference" -> Abductive
  Deductive -> "Modus ponens"
  Deductive -> "Modus tollens"
  Inductive -> "Generalisation"
  Inductive -> "Analogy"
  Abductive -> "Best explanation"
@/diagram

## 2. Validity Versus Soundness

**Validity** is a property of form, not content. A deductive argument
is **valid** when its premises cannot be true while its conclusion is
false; it is **sound** when, in addition, its premises are true.

Truth and validity are independent dimensions:

| Argument | Valid? | Premises true? |
| --- | --- | --- |
| All cats are mammals; all mammals are animals; so all cats are animals | Yes | Yes — sound |
| All cats are birds; all birds fly; so all cats fly | Yes | No — form holds, premise false |
| Some cats are black; some black things are dogs; so some cats are dogs | No | Yes — invalid despite true premises |

[important title="Truth is not validity"]
A valid argument can land on a false conclusion via a false premise; an
invalid one can stumble onto truth. Truth describes the world,
validity the wiring between claims.
[/important]

## 3. Four Valid Forms of Inference

Classical logic names forms that never carry us from true premises to a
false conclusion. Let **P** and **Q** stand for whole statements.

| Form | Pattern | Instance |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Modus ponens | If P then Q; P; so Q | If it rains the ground is wet; it rained; so the ground is wet |
| Modus tollens | If P then Q; not Q; so not P | If it rained the ground is wet; the ground is dry; so it did not rain |
| Hypothetical syllogism | If P then Q; if Q then R; so if P then R | If she studies she passes; if she passes she graduates |
| Disjunctive syllogism | P or Q; not P; so Q | The switch is on or off; it is not on; so it is off |

[tip]
Memory hook: in **modus ponens** you affirm the antecedent; in
**modus tollens** you deny the consequent — the reverse is the blunder.
[/tip]

## 4. Formal Fallacies

| Fallacy | Pattern | Why it fails |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Affirming the consequent | If P then Q; Q; so P | Q may have other causes |
| Denying the antecedent | If P then Q; not P; so not Q | Q may hold anyway |
| Undistributed middle | All A are C; all B are C; so all A are B | Sharing a property does not identify classes |

[example]
If the battery is dead, the car will not start. The car did not start;
therefore the battery is dead. Invalid — the fuel may be empty or the
starter broken. The form is affirming the consequent.
[/example]

## 5. Informal Fallacies

| Fallacy | What happens | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Ad hominem | Attacks the speaker, not the argument | "Of course she defends the tax — she is rich" |
| Straw man | Refutes a distorted version | Attacking a simplified stand-in for the real view |
| Appeal to authority | Cites prestige, not evidence | "A famous actor endorses it, so it works" |
| Bandwagon | Truth by popularity | "Millions believe it; it must be true" |
| False dilemma | Hides live options | "You are with us or against us" |
| Slippery slope | Denies any stopping point | "One exception today, chaos tomorrow" |
| Post hoc | After it, therefore because of it | The rooster crows, then the sun rises |
| Equivocation | Shifts a word's meaning midway | "A feather is light; what is light cannot be dark" |
| Begging the question | Assumes what it must prove | "This book is true because the book says so" |

## 6. Syllogisms, Venn Diagrams, and the Square

Aristotle's **categorical syllogism**: All M are P; all S are M; so
all S are P. **Venn diagrams** test it — shade the regions the premises
empty out; the form is valid when the shading forces the conclusion.

The **square of opposition** charts the logical relations among the
four basic statement shapes:

| Letter | Form | Name |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A | All S are P | Universal affirmative |
| E | No S are P | Universal negative |
| I | Some S are P | Particular affirmative |
| O | Some S are not P | Particular negative |

A and O are contradictories, as are E and I; A entails I, and E entails O.

## 7. Key Takeaways

| Concept | One-line meaning |
| --- | --- |
| Argument | Premises offered in support of a conclusion |
| Validity | True premises would guarantee a true conclusion |
| Soundness | Validity plus actually true premises |
| Deduction / induction / abduction | Necessity / probability / explanation |
| Formal fallacy | Invalid inferential pattern |
| Informal fallacy | Persuasive but defective move in context |

[summary]
Logic is topic-neutral — the same forms govern astronomy, law, and
gossip. An argument is premises supporting a conclusion; validity is
about form, soundness about fact; the valid forms and the fallacies
form the working toolkit of every subject that argues.
[/summary]
